WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Let me start by asking a question I love to hear the answers to.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Why are you here today?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
I know everyone has their own personal motive for taking a class like this.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Maybe you are starting a new job that requires it,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
or perhaps you are planning to start babysitting.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
You might be preparing for a new addition to the family,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
you are in the process of adopting a child,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
or you just have a general interest in being prepared.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Whatever your reason, I am so glad you have made the choice to be here

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
because this life-saving skill is absolutely invaluable during an emergency.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
The truth is, someone you know, someone you love,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
or just someone physically close to you

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.000
could suffer an emergency serious enough that their heart stops beating.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
When that heart stops, every single second counts

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
because the brain begins to die almost immediately.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.000
By starting CPR without delay,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
you are vastly increasing that person's chance of surviving.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Now, it is important for us to acknowledge a sobering reality.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Statistically, the majority of people who receive CPR do not survive.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
This is often because there is a delay between when the heart stops

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.000
and when CPR actually begins.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
That is exactly why your training today is so critical.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
You are learning how to be the one who acts fast and bridges that gap,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
providing high-quality care when seconds count the most.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Our goal today is to make sure you're ready to handle a life-threatening emergency,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
and that starts with knowing the difference between a heart attack and cardiac arrest.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.000
It helps to think of it as a plumbing issue versus an electrical issue.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
A heart attack happens when a pipe or artery gets blocked,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000
which starves the heart muscle of blood.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.000
In this situation, the person is usually still awake and talking,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.000
but they might feel chest pain, nausea, or shortness of breath.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The heart is still beating, but it's in serious trouble,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
so you need to call 911 immediately.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Cardiac arrest, on the other hand, is an electrical problem.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.000
This is when the heart's power suddenly goes out and it stops pumping entirely.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Because the blood stops flowing, the person will collapse,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.000
become unresponsive, and stop breathing normally.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
This is the moment when you have to act fast.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.000
You need to call 911, start CPR,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.000
and use an AED right away to try and jumpstart the heart's rhythm.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.000
To keep it simple, just remember that if a person is awake but hurting,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
it might be a heart attack and they need an ambulance.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000
If they've collapsed and stopped breathing,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
it may be cardiac arrest and they need CPR immediately.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
So what exactly is CPR?

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The letters stand for cardiopulmonary resuscitation.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Cardio refers to the heart, pulmonary refers to the lungs,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
and resuscitation simply means bringing someone back to life or reviving them.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
In its simplest form, CPR is our way of manually doing the heart's job

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.000
when it has stopped beating.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
It is an emergency procedure we use during cardiac arrest to save lives.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.000
By combining chest compressions with rescue breaths,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.000
we are literally pumping blood and oxygen through the body

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.000
to keep vital organs like the brain alive until professional help gets there.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:24.000
This is so important because effective CPR maintains blood flow,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
which significantly increases the chance of survival

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.000
and a good recovery for the brain.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Now, when do you actually start?

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
You only begin CPR when a person is unresponsive and not breathing normally

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.000
or if they are only gasping.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
If you see that, you need to act fast.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.000
It might sound like a lot, but don't worry.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.000
We are going to practice this together until it feels like second nature.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
When will you actually need to use these skills?

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.000
The reality is that most of us will probably never need to perform CPR,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.000
but we are here today to make sure you are prepared if that moment ever comes.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
This class could be the difference between life and death for a family member,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
a co-worker, or even a stranger you happen to be with.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.000
If you are there when someone suddenly collapses

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.000
and you find that they do not respond and are not breathing normally,

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.000
that is your cue to start CPR immediately.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
It is important to know that not breathing normally

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
means they are not taking steady, regular breaths.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Occasional gasping is not normal breathing.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.000
It is actually a sign of a serious emergency called agonal breaths,

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.000
and it means someone needs to take action right away.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
It is completely natural to feel a bit of worry or even a lot of pressure

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.000
when thinking about performing CPR in a real emergency.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.000
I want to address some of the what-if questions

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.000
that might be playing in the back of your mind right now.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
One big fear people often have is causing an injury like breaking a person's ribs.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
While that can happen, it is important to remember that these are survivable injuries.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Doing nothing can be more harmful than doing something.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.000
If a person is in cardiac arrest,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
the alternative to your help is far worse than a few broken ribs.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Another common hesitation is about giving rescue breaths.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.000
If you are hesitant to help because you do not have a mask or barrier device handy,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.000
consider doing compressions only CPR,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
as it can still be more beneficial than doing nothing at all.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.000
However, it is important to remember that unlike adults,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.000
most pediatric cardiac arrests are secondary to respiratory failure or asphyxia.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Research and scientific guidelines emphasize that pediatric resuscitation

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
requires both compressions and ventilations.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.000
You might also worry about doing the steps wrong or not being perfect.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Please do not let that stop you.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Even if your technique is not perfect,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:51.000
taking action can still greatly increase the chances of survival for someone in cardiac arrest.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Just focus on pushing hard and fast in the center of the chest.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.000
We are going to practice together several times today,

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.000
so you feel that muscle memory kick in.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000
If you are worried about the legal side of things,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:09.000
remember that most areas have Good Samaritan laws in place.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.000
The Good Samaritan law is a legal principle designed to protect you

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.000
when you give emergency help to someone who is hurt or in danger.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
Its main goal is to protect bystanders like you

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.000
who give reasonable emergency assistance from legal liability

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.000
so they feel safe helping someone in danger.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.000
While all 50 states have a version of this law,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.000
the specific details can vary.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.000
If you want to know the exact rules for your area,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
you can always check your local state laws or the Attorney General's website.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Finally, I want to share an important piece of research.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:51.000
In the United States, women are actually 14% less likely to receive bystander CPR than men.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.000
This isn't because women's hearts are different.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.000
It is because bystanders often hesitate.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.000
People sometimes worry about touching a woman's chest,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.000
fear potential accusations,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.000
or are just uncertain if it is truly cardiac arrest.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.000
The best way to move past that hesitation is education.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.000
As we increase the number of individuals who know CPR,

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.000
we can begin to normalize providing life-saving help to every single person,

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.000
regardless of their gender.

